landing-review: landing 11-5c (tagpu_terr.c loses its GL), level high, 2026-09-19

Range main...HEAD at b5cdfe1..a91e51f for the first round and a0026b3 alone for
the re-review; main at b5cdfe1. High rather than medium because the landing
changes a published predicate the consumer's shader reads, and the brief
(wrongly, see M-thread) treated the hand-over as crossing the thread split.

THREE dedicated read-only reviewers in two rounds: one on deletion safety and
comment truth, one on behaviour and lifetime, then one on the fix commit alone.
No finding applied without verifying it against the code first.

ROUND 1 -- 23 findings, two HIGH which are one defect, AND IT IS ONE THIS
LANDING INTRODUCED.

H1/H2 -- making `restored` honest fired the CONSUMER's parity rule for the
first time. tagpu_vk_terr.c stood the whole terrain pass down whenever a
restore request stood that this lane had not painted. Right while a GL twin
drew beside us; there is no twin. And every way the restorer can decline
latches s_rjTried one-way for the device's life, after which `have` can never
become 1 -- so the stand-down was PERMANENT: no terrain at all for the session,
said once and silent after. tagpu_vk_restore.h promises such a refusal leaves
Classic++ indexed, "the shipped fallback, not a fault". Fixed where the fact
lives: uRestored is now t.restored && s_rgbAtlas.view && .have, so a request
this lane cannot service draws INDEXED.

M -- the stale restored atlas past a serial change: every give-up path now
drops `have`, at the give-up sites and not up front, because `repaint` reads it.
M -- the repaint throttle (found by BOTH round-1 reviewers independently): HEAD
gated it on state 2, "the job is idle", a completion this side cannot see.
Collapsing the state machine made it fire per palette change, and pal_serial
bumps once a frame during a fade, so the restore made no progress for its
length. Now gated on the palette having settled.
M-thread -- THE BRIEF WAS WRONG: there is no thread split in this pass.
Producer and consumer run in one iteration of the render loop, job_add COPIES
the frames, tagpu_reclaim does not apply. Recorded in gpu-status and the header.
M -- two counting/scoping errors in my own §2.71 ("glsl_begin occurs three
times" was four, and the fourth was the stale comment the miscount hid; "every
GL site sat under !tagpu_vk_owns_present()" is false for 17 of 107, which went
through a SECOND guard on a GL object name).
M -- the !s_hVao removal was written as though it opened the caster route. It
did not: tagpu_shadow_hills has no callers and would return at !s_live.
M -- terrain's half of the tagpu_restoredump.on byte oracle went with
dump_if_armed; ta-drive's loop looked like four passes and covered three.
Plus ten stale comments naming deleted GL as live, and the header contracts.

ROUND 2 (the fix commit) -- no HIGH, main fix confirmed, two real defects IN
THE FIXES.

M -- the restored atlas-width bound DID NOT BIND: it set s_maxTex = m and
bailed, inside a block conditioned on s_maxTex != tagpu_vk_max_image_dim(), so
the block was skipped from the next frame on. The pre-11-5c original latched
s_state = 2. Now the refusal leaves s_maxTex alone and the LOG latches, on the
device's reported value.
L-but-real -- removing the stand-down let the pass draw while binding 42 named
an image still in VK_IMAGE_LAYOUT_UNDEFINED, on exactly the declined-restorer
path the fix exists for. Binding 42 now names the restored view only when
`.have`, which cannot precede the job's clear -- so the test IS "it has left
UNDEFINED", and the flag and the descriptor now come from the same two facts.
M -- the debounce comment overclaimed: the render loop wakes more often than
the palette changes, so a slow fade can still republish per step. What would
close it is a completion signal the hand-over does not carry; said so in code.
Plus a second comment-truth pass: six cross-references to the deleted refusal,
the header's `restored` contract, the glreset caller chain, the failure log
line, and the retained-list units (KiB, and a device-limit caveat).

